  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-2 mb-4">The backward calendar</h2>
  <p>
    Take the date the goods must be on shelf and subtract. Clearance and inland delivery 7–10 days, sea freight 20–35
    days depending on the lane, production 15–25 days, plus the time it takes you to approve a sample and pay a deposit.
    That total is usually between ten and twelve weeks — and it does not include the time you spend deciding.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason seasonal orders fail is almost never the factory. It is a buyer who starts the process twelve weeks out and
    expects a six-week result, then pays for air freight and gives away the margin. If your window is tight, the honest
    options are air freight, a smaller sea shipment now plus a second later, or a different product. Not a promise that
    production can be compressed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">Chinese New Year is the fixed constraint</h2>
  <p>
    Every supplier in Chenghai shares the same holiday. Production stops, workers travel, and the return is staggered over
    the following weeks. Freight space tightens on both sides of it. If a season starts in March or April, the goods must
    be finished and booked before the holiday, which means the order is placed in the preceding autumn — the same time
    everyone else is competing for the same container slots.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">Seasonal packaging without seasonal MOQ</h2>
  <p>
    A themed box is the cheapest way to make a generic product seasonal, and the most common way to blow up a budget. The
    toy has a MOQ; the printed box has its own. Two routes work: a printed sleeve over the stock box, which needs a lower
    print quantity than a full box, or a sticker or hang tag, which needs almost none. Ask for all three options priced
    against your quantity rather than assuming the box is the only answer.
  </p>

